title: math_context
---
// Math Town dialogue. Node titles are referenced by town placements.
// Canter's freight-manifest choice must set orbital_manifest_signed.
// This context node is not placed in the world.
===

title: erdish
---
Erdish: My mind is open.
-> What are you looking at?
    Erdish: The grain in this table. Follow it long enough and you get to the window.
    Erdish: Almost.
-> Did you climb in through the window?
    Erdish: Yes. Emmy says I mustn't do it again.
-> Are you waiting for someone?
    Erdish: I had a question for you. I've forgotten what it was.
    Erdish: I'll find you when it comes back.
-> Leave him alone.
===

title: trent
---
Trent: Hey. You new here? I'm Trent.
-> Just got in.
    Trent: Right. Well, Emmy does food, Walter does the gate records. Try not to mix them up. Walter gets touchy.
-> Where can I get something to eat?
    Trent: Cafe on the corner. Tell Emmy I sent you. Actually, don't. I still owe her for breakfast.
-> Who deals with the station?
    Trent: Canter, at the surveyors' office. He sends the survey equipment.
    Trent: You'll know it's him. He's usually buried in paperwork.
-> See you around.
    Trent: Yeah. See you.
===

title: walter
---
Walter: Hang on. I haven't put you in the book.
-> What book?
    Walter: Gate arrivals. Name, time, destination. Nothing complicated.
-> Is something wrong?
    Walter: Two unsigned notes came through this week. Both said, "Weather is fine."
    Walter: Same ink, too. I don't like it.
-> Maybe somebody's writing home.
    Walter: Then they can sign their name.
-> Does the station have a gate log too?
    Walter: Of course. Officer Okafor has a book up there.
    Walter: I only record what passes through this gate.
-> I'll come back.
    Walter: Don't leave through the gate without telling me.
===

title: busy_beaver
---
Busy Beaver: Move back a bit. I need to get this beam straight.
-> What's it for?
    Busy Beaver: Gate cradle. The old bracket cracked, and now nothing lines up.
-> How long until it's done?
    Busy Beaver: Don't know. I ran the test all night. It's still going.
    Busy Beaver: I thought it would've stopped by now.
-> Why did the bracket crack?
    Busy Beaver: Haven't worked that out. We've had more traffic, but that doesn't prove anything.
    Busy Beaver: Don't throw the old bracket away. I still need it.
-> Need a hand?
    Busy Beaver: Hold that end. No, the other one.
    Busy Beaver: There. Don't move.
-> I'll get out of the way.
    Busy Beaver: Good. Thanks.
===

title: peggy
---
Peggy: You're blocking the west door. Can you stand over there?
-> What are you doing?
    Peggy: Victor picks a door. I go behind the wall, then come out where he tells me.
    Peggy: He doesn't get to see how I do it.
-> Why won't you show him the passage?
    Peggy: Because then he'd know it. That's not what we're testing.
-> I'll move.
    Peggy: Thanks. West door, Victor! I heard you.
===

title: victor
---
Victor: Twenty-seven. She's managed it twenty-seven times.
-> So she knows a way through.
    Victor: I believe she does. I still want another few rounds.
-> Why not follow her?
    Victor: She won't let me. I can hardly blame her.
-> I'll leave you to it.
    Victor: West door again, Peggy.
===

title: data_lovelace
---
Data Lovelace: Sorry, could you keep clear of the cards? I've got them in order.
-> What are they for?
    Data Lovelace: Instructions for the engine. It does the numbers, then the loom weaves the result.
-> What are you making?
    Data Lovelace: Flowers. Supposed to be flowers, anyway. It keeps weaving little squares.
    Data Lovelace: I think I've got two rows backward.
-> Couldn't you draw the flowers yourself?
    Data Lovelace: I can draw one. I need the loom to make fifty.
-> Sorry. I'll leave them alone.
    Data Lovelace: Thank you.
===

title: olivia
---
Olivia: Yes?
-> Are you the oracle?
    Olivia: People call me that.
-> Can the gate take me home?
    Olivia: Where's home?
    Olivia: If you don't know the address, I can't give you the route.
-> Never mind.
    Olivia: All right.
===

title: leib_knives
---
Gottfried Leib-Knives: Stay back. Blade's sharp.
-> Who are you fighting?
    Gottfried Leib-Knives: Nobody. My partner went home. I'm practicing my distance.
-> What are the marks on the ground?
    Gottfried Leib-Knives: Where the tip lands at each step. I'm measuring the rate of change.
    Gottfried Leib-Knives: If you want to watch, stand behind me.
-> Have you heard of Gnu-ton?
    Gottfried Leib-Knives: He's the one with the gravity works at the station, isn't he?
    Gottfried Leib-Knives: I heard he fights from a giant's shoulders. I wouldn't know where to stand.
-> I'll leave you to it.
    Gottfried Leib-Knives: Much obliged.
===

title: hypatia_prime
---
Hypatia: Don't lean on the mount. It's loose.
-> What are you looking for?
    Hypatia: A light near the station. It was there last night. Now I can't find it.
    Hypatia: It moved, then stopped. I want another look.
-> Could it be from the gravity works?
    Hypatia: Maybe. They're close enough.
    Hypatia: I need to see it again before I start blaming anyone.
-> Could it be something on the lens?
    Hypatia: Davy keeps asking me that. I've cleaned it.
-> They say you're a reconstruction.
    Hypatia: They say it to my face, too.
    Hypatia: I've got the notebooks. I don't have everything she knew.
-> I'll let you work.
    Hypatia: Mm.
===

title: emmy_noether_cafe
---
Emmy Ethereal: Sit wherever you like. Except there. Erdish has taken over that table.
-> Can I get some coffee?
    Emmy Ethereal: In a minute. Kettle's still on.
-> What's the writing on the board?
    Emmy Ethereal: A proof I was working on last night. Haven't had time to rub it out.
    Emmy Ethereal: The short version? If the rules don't change with time, you get conservation of energy.
-> Does Erdish always come in through the window?
    Emmy Ethereal: Only when I forget to lock it.
-> I'll find a seat.
    Emmy Ethereal: If you want lunch, order before I close the kitchen.
===

title: georg_canter
---
<<if condition("world.flag_set", "orbital_manifest_signed")>>
    Georg Canter: I've signed it. Twelve lenses. Three copies. I hope that's the end of it.
    -> Why did you write aleph-null in the first place?
        Georg Canter: I thought the customs officer might find it funny.
        Georg Canter: She did not.
    -> What were the lenses for?
        Georg Canter: A survey ship. They're mapping a moon without a gate.
    -> What are you working on now?
        Georg Canter: Lists. Give me a list of every infinite string of zeros and ones, and I'll find one you've missed.
    -> I'll let you get on.
<<elseif condition("world.flag_set", "orbital_consignment_query")>>
    Georg Canter: Ah. The station sent you about crate 0447, didn't they?
    -> They need you to sign for twelve lenses.
        <<command "world.set_flag" "orbital_manifest_signed" true>>
        Georg Canter: Yes, fine. Twelve. There, I've signed it.
        Georg Canter: Tell customs I'm sorry about the quantity box.
    -> Why did you put aleph-null on the form?
        Georg Canter: It was a joke. A rather poor one, apparently.
    -> Where are the lenses going?
        Georg Canter: To a survey ship at the station. They need them to map a moon.
    -> What's all this paper?
        Georg Canter: I'm trying to put numbers in a list. The interesting ones won't stay put.
    -> I'll come back.
<<else>>
    Georg Canter: Careful with that stack. I've numbered every page.
    -> What are you counting?
        Georg Canter: Mostly I'm trying to show what you can't count this way.
        Georg Canter: An endless list still can't hold every infinite string of zeros and ones.
    -> Is that a shipping form?
        Georg Canter: Twelve survey lenses for the orbital station.
        Georg Canter: I wrote something stupid in the quantity box. They'll probably send it back.
        Georg Canter: The survey ship's waiting on them.
    -> Don't you have enough numbers already?
        Georg Canter: I haven't even started.
    -> I'll leave you to it.
<<endif>>
===

title: davy_hylbert
---
Davy Hylbert: Sorry, we're full tonight.
-> I thought you always had room.
    Davy Hylbert: In the infinite hotel, yes. This one's got twelve rooms.
    Davy Hylbert: I might have a cot. Depends if Hypatia needs it.
-> How does the infinite hotel work?
    Davy Hylbert: Move everyone from room one to two, two to three, and so on. First room's free.
    Davy Hylbert: Don't ask me to do that here. People have unpacked.
-> Why's the observatory still lit?
    Davy Hylbert: Hypatia's up there. Something moving near the station, she says.
    Davy Hylbert: I brought her dinner yesterday. She'd forgotten to eat.
-> I'll come another time.
    Davy Hylbert: Right. Sorry.
===

title: hotel_monty_carlo
---
Monty Carlo: Welcome, welcome, WELCOME to the ballroom! Three doors! One car! Two goats! And one lucky robot!
-> How does it work?
    Monty Carlo: You pick a door. I, who know everything, open another one and show you a goat. Then I ask: do you want to switch?
    Monty Carlo: And you say yes. Because switching wins two times in three. Everybody says yes. Eventually.
-> Why does switching win?
    Monty Carlo: Your first pick is right one time in three. I never open the car. So the other door holds every case where you were wrong. Two in three!
    Monty Carlo: A mathematician wrote in to say I was lying. Hundreds of them. I sent each of them a goat.
-> What happens to the goats?
    Monty Carlo: They live here. Hylbert gives each one a room. There are always rooms. The goats have never been happier.
-> I'll stay where I am.
    Monty Carlo: Staying! Bold! Wrong, two times in three, but bold!
===

title: hotel_paradox_barber
---
The Paradox Barber: Hylbert gave me a room for the shop. Room n plus two. Then a guest arrived and I was moved to n plus three.
-> Do you shave the guests?
    The Paradox Barber: Every guest who does not shave himself. There are infinitely many of them. I am behind.
    The Paradox Barber: Hylbert says I can move each customer one chair down and the first chair is always free. The first chair is never free.
-> Who shaves you?
    The Paradox Barber: The hotel has a policy on that question. The policy is a set that does not contain itself.
-> I will let you get on.
    The Paradox Barber: Mind the foam. It is countable.
===

title: hotel_joseph_furrier
---
Furrier: The corridor goes on for ever and the heating does not. Shut the door behind you.
-> Which room are you in?
    Furrier: Every few minutes, the next one. Hylbert keeps making room. My luggage is a sum of infinitely many moves.
    Furrier: I can tell you my room's frequency, or its number. Never both. Heisenberg is down the corridor; he is not sure where.
-> Why are you staying here?
    Furrier: A conference on heat. In a corridor with no end, it never reaches the far wall. Ideal conditions.
-> Stay warm.
    Furrier: I intend to. Converge, if you can.
===

title: hotel_python_goras
---
Python Goras: Room service sent beans. I sent them back. Room service moved me to the next room.
-> What's wrong with beans?
    Python Goras: Brotherhood rule. Older than the hotel. Older than infinity, which in this corridor is saying something.
-> Do you like the hotel?
    Python Goras: Every room is square. I measured the diagonal of mine. The number would not stop. I have asked them not to mention it to the others.
-> Enjoy your stay.
    Python Goras: The stay enjoys itself. I merely remain in it, in a new room every so often.
===

title: zeno_halfway
---
Zeno Halfway: Good morning! Or afternoon. I set out for the reservoir at dawn. Some years ago. I'm making excellent progress.
-> How far have you got?
    Zeno Halfway: Halfway. Before that I was halfway to here. Tomorrow I'll be halfway from here. Every day, half of what's left.
    Zeno Halfway: You'd think I'd be there by now. You'd be thinking like a person who has never done the sum.
-> Why not just walk the rest?
    Zeno Halfway: To walk the rest I'd first have to walk half the rest. And before that, half of that. I've got a list. It is very long.
-> Will you ever arrive?
    Zeno Halfway: In the limit, yes! The sign at the dam says so. I've read it from here. Through a telescope.
    Zeno Halfway: An arrow I fired at the reservoir in my youth is still in the air somewhere. We keep each other company.
-> Good luck.
    Zeno Halfway: Thank you. I'll be halfway to grateful by tomorrow.
===

title: mine_kleinbottle
---
Felix Kleinbottle: Mind your head. Or your feet. Down here it depends which way you're facing.
-> What do you mine?
    Felix Kleinbottle: The seam. It's got one side, which is a great saving. You dig along it, you come back along it, and you never once had to go round.
-> What is the half twist?
    Felix Kleinbottle: The far end. Press it and the mine turns over. The floor's the ceiling, the ceiling's the floor, and the coins on the ceiling are suddenly underfoot.
    Felix Kleinbottle: Press the other one, on the other side, to come back. It's the same switch. Don't think about it too long.
-> Why "Kleinbottle"?
    Felix Kleinbottle: Family name. We used to make bottles with no inside. Hard to sell. You'd pour, and pour, and the bottle would agree with you.
-> I'll go and twist.
    Felix Kleinbottle: Lovely. Wave when you're upside down. I'll wave back. It'll look the same to both of us.
===

title: hanoi_lucas
---
Brother Lucas: Welcome to the Tower. Mind the disks; they're gold and they're counted. One move at a time, never a larger on a smaller.
-> What happens when the last disk is moved?
    Brother Lucas: The world ends. That's the legend. I wrote it. It sold a great many puzzles.
    Brother Lucas: Sixty-four disks takes eighteen quintillion moves. At a move a second, the sun goes out first. I find that very restful.
-> How many moves so far?
    Brother Lucas: One million, forty-eight thousand, five hundred and seventy-five. It's on the wall. Every stroke is a move. The wall is very full.
-> Can I move one?
    Brother Lucas: You can stand on them. Moving them is my job, and I'm in no hurry. Nobody who understands the puzzle is ever in a hurry.
-> I'll leave you to it.
    Brother Lucas: Thank you. I'll get back to it presently. Presently is a very long word here.
===

title: meadow_fibbs
---
Farmer Fibbs: Mind the plots. Each one is the last two put together, and they don't like being stepped on any more than they like being counted.
-> How many rabbits do you have?
    Farmer Fibbs: This month? Last month's, plus the month before's. Started with a pair. Never sold one. Never will catch up.
    Farmer Fibbs: The sixth hutch burst at eight. I'm building a thirteen. Then a twenty-one. I've stopped building and started fencing.
-> Why are the plots square?
    Farmer Fibbs: One, one, two, three, five, eight. Lay them side by side and draw a quarter-circle in each and you get a spiral, if you squint, and unroll it. My grandmother did it in a ring. Took the whole valley.
-> What about the sunflowers?
    Farmer Fibbs: Seeds go in at the golden angle. Not my doing; they do it themselves. I asked one why once. It looked at me with about four hundred faces.
-> And the mites?
    Farmer Fibbs: They divide. Everything else here adds. They're the wrong kind of growth entirely. Mind your ankles past the hutch.
===

title: woods_ivo
---
Ivo: Careful on the path. It's a lot longer than it looks, if you look closely.
-> What do you do here?
    Ivo: Groundskeeper. I trim the hedge, I sweep the leaves, I count the trees. The counting is the hard part.
    Ivo: Every tree is made of smaller trees. I counted to a thousand on one branch and it had only just started.
-> Why is the hedge that shape?
    Ivo: I started trimming it flat in the spring. Took a third off every edge and put a point on the middle. Then I did it again.
    Ivo: Third iteration now. It's longer than the woods. It's still the same width. Nobody believes me.
-> What's the bush at the far end?
    Ivo: The Mandelbrot. Don't prune it. Every bit you cut off has a little copy of the whole thing on it, and they all want watering.
-> I'll leave you to it.
    Ivo: Mind the slugs. They see dogs in everything.
===
